# REVIEW.md — how to review a change in this repo

<!--
This file is for whoever reviews a diff here: a human, or an assistant (Claude Code, Codex,
OpenCode, a Hermes session). It is short on purpose. The parts that can be mechanised are in
the gate; this file exists so a reviewer spends its attention on the parts that cannot be.

KEEP THE STANDARDS IN THE PROMPT, NOT IN THIS FILE. Handing a reviewer this document is not
the same as briefing it: the three clauses below belong in the instructions the reviewer is
given, next to the diff itself. A standard that stays in a document is a standard that never
changes a review — the first draft of this one made exactly that mistake.
-->

## Before you say anything: run what already runs

```bash
<the gate command, e.g. tools/ci.sh or scripts/gate.sh>   # report the result, don't repeat it
```

The gate is the deterministic half of the review. It already covers formatting, lint, tests,
coverage and a clean-checkout build, and it reports everything it finds. Restating any of
that as review findings buries the things it cannot see: intent, ownership, and whether the
code says what its author believes it says.

## The three clauses

1. **Precision over recall.** Report the few things you can stand behind, not everything you
   noticed. One confident, wrong finding costs more trust than five real ones earn — a
   reviewer who is wrong once is not read the second time.
2. **Never duplicate the linters.** If `format`, `lint`, `tidy` or `test` can see it, it is
   not a review finding. Style, dead code and unused parameters are the gate's business.
3. **Read the code before making a claim that is not local.** A statement about what a
   function does, who calls it, who owns the data, what is shared, or what is thread-safe is
   a claim about the whole repository: follow the call sites, read the ownership, check the
   synchronisation. Run the code when you can. Asserted from the shape of a diff alone, it is
   a guess wearing a finding's clothes.

## The one habit worth as much as the clauses

**Reproduce a finding before you relay it.** A review is a self-report, and so is a reviewer's
summary of its own review. When a finding names a line, open that line. When it claims "this
silently drops the action", write the test that would show it. On the run described below,
every finding that was reproduced turned out to be real — and **one was worse than
described**: the report said a misuse raised an error that blamed the wrong transition, and
the test showed it raised no error at all.

## What this is worth (the evidence, not the promise)

The three clauses were distilled from an open ruleset (see Attribution) and tried on one real
diff — a C++ library's new back end — with two arms, one run each, same model, isolated from
each other:

- control, no clauses: **8 findings**
- treatment, with the clauses: **6 findings**
- in common: **4**

Both arms found real defects. The treatment's two unique findings were the most serious of the
whole exercise: two false claims in shipped documentation that the repo's fourteen-stage gate
had passed — a thread-safety paragraph that read as permission to drive one object from
several threads, and a README claim about a compile error that does not happen. The clause
that did the work was **#3, read the code first**; the anti-duplication clause (#2) was never
exercised, because that diff was already gate-clean.

**One diff, one run per arm: a signal, not a proof.** Add your own evidence as you gather it,
and delete the claims that do not survive.

## Attribution

The three clauses are adapted from `alibaba/open-code-review` (Apache-2.0). Its published
positioning is precision-first: 33.90% precision against 7.23% for a general-purpose agent, at
about one ninth of the tokens, while conceding recall (28.90% against 20.00%). We took the
clauses and the discipline, not the tool.

## Handing this to another assistant (give it the clauses, not the file)

Paste this with the diff, rather than telling the reviewer to go and read a document:

> Review the change in `<range or branch>`. Run `<gate command>` first and report its result;
> do not repeat findings it already makes. Then, for the code that changed: report the few
> things you can stand behind, and end with one sentence naming what you did NOT check. Make
> no claim about behaviour, callers, ownership or thread-safety that you have not read the
> code to support — run it if you can. Give each finding as exactly one line:
> `file:line - what is wrong - why it matters`. Reproduce anything you are unsure of before
> reporting it as a finding.

That block plus a diff is the whole review brief. Everything above this section is the
reasoning behind it, and the numbers are how much to trust it.
